  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Stable conditions cause valley fog and low clouds through\n  Friday. \n\n- A pattern shift on Saturday will bring some atmospheric river\n  remnants to northwest Montana with higher snow levels and\n  minimal impacts expected. \n\n- Cooling early next week with lowering snow levels.\n\nClearing overnight may allow for some radiational cooling and\nreintroduction of valley fog this morning. Satellite is already\nshowing decent coverage of fog and stratus in some valleys in\ncentral Idaho between the cloud breaks, and cameras are showing\nfog near Missoula and Kalispell this morning. \n\nPacific moisture wraps around a powerful Pacific low pressure\nsystem as it drops south along the west coast Friday. While it\nwill bring a warm southerly flow, it will also increase clouds\nwhich in turn will limit warming Friday and Saturday (even limited\nwarming will run 10 degrees above normal in many locations). A\nlittle light precipitation is expected in north-central Idaho and\nLemhi County, ID Saturday with no impacts expected. \n\nThe next push of moisture arrives late Monday affecting the region\nthrough Wednesday morning. Precipitation amounts are light in most\nensemble members but, there is a minority of members that have\nvery high precipitation amounts exceeding 1 inch in the mountains.\nAs is typical for this time of year, snow levels are very\nuncertain with recent model trends going higher. As a result,\nensembles are now showing a less than 10% chance for 6-inches or\nmore of snow in the Anaconda Pintlar mountains by Wednesday\nmorning. While impacts should be limited to near the passes, they\nwill also dissipate rapidly as the sun rises Wednesday. If\nprecipitation amounts continue to trend up, impacts could increase\nand expand, stay updated on future forecasts, especially if you\nplan to head into the back country next week.",
